description:
Driver Verifier's low-resources simulation makes allocations fail to exercise error paths. Do it systematically: run an initialisation function once for **each** allocation it makes, failing exactly that one, and check that every failure path cleans up.

```cpp
class Allocator
{
public:
    explicit Allocator(int failAt);      // the failAt-th allocation (0-based) fails; -1: none fail
    int allocate(std::size_t bytes);     // a handle > 0, or 0 on failure
    void free(int handle);
    int outstanding() const;             // allocated and not freed
    int calls() const;                   // allocate calls made
};

using Init = std::function<bool(Allocator&, std::vector<int>& kept)>;
std::string checkFailurePaths(const Init& init);
```

`init` must return `false` and free everything when an allocation fails; when it succeeds it returns `true` and puts the handles it keeps in `kept` (as `DriverEntry` keeps its device and buffers).

`checkFailurePaths`:
1. Run `init` with no failures. It must succeed, otherwise return `"fails without injected failures"`. Count its allocation calls `n`. Free everything in `kept`; if anything is still outstanding, return `"leak on success"`.
2. For `i` from 0 to `n - 1`: run `init` with allocation `i` failing. It must return false (`"succeeded although allocation <i> failed"`) and leave nothing outstanding (`"leak when allocation <i> fails"`).
3. Return `"all <n> failure paths clean"`.

